d slam doubles titles from 2009 Wimbledon through 2010 Roland Garros, which would catapult them to co-No.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" 1 doubles players on 7 June 2010.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" Two weeks later, on 21 June 2010, Serena would hold the No. 1 singles ranking and Venus would be right behind her at No. 2 in singles.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" Their most recent grand slam doubles titles came at the 2012 Wimbledon & 2016 Wimbledon events.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":11,"text":" They remain very close, often watching each other's matches in support, even after one of them has been knocked out of a tournament.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Mariaan de Swardt (born 18 March 1971) is a former tennis player from South Africa, who played as a professional from 1988 to 2001.","title":"Mariaan de Swardt"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" She twice represented her native country at the Summer Olympics, in 1992 and 1996, and was a member of the South African Fed Cup Team in 1992 and 1994–1997.","title":"Mariaan de Swardt"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In 2006, de Swardt became a U.S. citizen.","title":"Mariaan de Swardt"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 1999 Wimbledon Championships – Women's Doubles was the women's doubles event of the hundred-and-thirteenth edition of Wimbledon, the third Grand Slam of the year, the most prestigious tournament on grass courts, and the oldest tennis tournament in the world.","title":"1999 Wimbledon Championships – Women's Doubles"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Martina Hingis and Jana Novotná were the defending champions but only Novotná competed that year with Natasha Zvereva.","title":"1999 Wimbledon Championships – Women's Doubles"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Novotná and Zvereva lost in the semifinals to Mariaan de Swardt and Elena Tatarkova.","title":"1999 Wimbledon Championships – Women's Doubles"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Mariaan de Swardt won in the final 3–6, 7–6, 7–5 against Barbara Schett.","title":"1998 Boston Cup – Singles"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Lisa Raymond and Rennae Stubbs won in the final 6–4, 6–4 against Mariaan de Swardt and Mary Joe Fernández.","title":"1998 Boston Cup – Doubles"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Serena Williams has won 23 Grand Slam singles titles, held the world No. 1 ranking, and captured multiple Wimbledon singles crowns, while Mariaan de Swardt’s record includes a single WTA singles title and a semifinal appearance in a Grand Slam doubles event.
